## Flight report

- **Left gate**: crossed cleanly during the k=3 override (straight advance along heading after the downward panel showed the crossbar passing underneath and the forward view showed the "just crossed" one-post-at-edge signature). Crossing was through the middle — both posts had been symmetric and centered before the final straight-in move.
- **Post-left-gate maneuver**: executed as planned — one straight +x move (k=4) to clear the gate line, avoiding the policy's proposed reverse/turn-toward-table that would have clipped the post.
- **Center gate**: never crossed. After turning to search for it (k=5–7), both posts and a centered gap came into clear view at k=8 (heading ≈ -78°). The subsequent straight advance along that heading, however, led not to the gate but to a large white pillar/wall structure (no yellow/teal — not a gate) fronting what looked like an enclosed office/window room. Repeated turns and a short advance (k=9–12) never recovered a clean two-post view; I likely lost the correct approach line after the k=8 advance, and ran out of budget while trying to relocate it.
- **Penguin**: never located — no black-and-white shape was seen in the downward camera at any point.
- **Final pose**: x=2.45, y=-1.89, z=1.53, heading ≈ -62°, stationary next to the pillar/office area.
- **Outcome**: incomplete. Only the left gate was crossed (cleanly, through the middle); the center gate and the penguin hover were not achieved. I stopped on the final decision rather than push forward blindly near the unidentified obstacle with no target sighted.
